package com.bqsqwad.heartbreakhealer.feature.entertainment

/** Five original ten-part serials; each mystery is resolved in its final part. */
internal object StoryLibraryBatch10 {
    val stories = listOf(
        StorySeries("the_clockmakers_guest","The Clockmaker's Guest","A quiet workshop holds the key to a town's missing minutes.", listOf(
            StoryPart(1,"Seven Minutes Late",listOf("Every clock in Mr Okafor's workshop ran seven minutes slow. He insisted it was deliberate, though he never explained why. When Ada came to collect her repaired watch, she found a note tucked beneath it: 'Ask who borrowed the afternoon.'","The note was signed with a tiny drawing of a blue bird. Ada recognised it from the old station clock, which had stopped on the day the town's railway museum closed.")),
            StoryPart(2,"The Museum Ledger",listOf("Ada asked the museum curator for the final visitor ledger. One entry had been crossed out, but its pressure marks remained: a visitor named L. Mensah, recorded at 3:07 p.m.","The curator remembered a retired timetable clerk who had spent years trying to preserve the station's original clock. Ada found his address in the town directory.")),
            StoryPart(3,"A Clerk's Memory",listOf("Mr Mensah welcomed Ada with a box of carefully labelled photographs. He explained that the station clock had once been seven minutes ahead, helping passengers avoid missing trains during a period of unreliable announcements.","When the railway modernised, the clock was reset. The old setting had been part of a community agreement, but the paperwork had disappeared. Mr Mensah gave Ada a photograph showing the clockmaker beside a blue bird emblem.")),
            StoryPart(4,"The Bird on the Window",listOf("The emblem belonged to a small repair guild that had maintained public clocks without charge. Ada found its minutes in the library, including a record of a clock adjustment made on the afternoon the museum closed.","The record named Mr Okafor and listed a second witness. The witness was a former station manager, now living in a nearby care residence.")),
            StoryPart(5,"The Witness",listOf("The former manager remembered the decision clearly. The seven-minute difference had been kept temporarily while the station tested a new announcement system. The test ended, but nobody documented the final reset.","He also remembered that the museum's closing display included a signed clock face. It had been moved to storage, not discarded. He gave Ada the storage inventory number.")),
            StoryPart(6,"The Painted Face",listOf("At the municipal store, Ada and the curator found the clock face wrapped in cloth. On its back were signatures from the clockmaker, the station staff, and the guild volunteers.","A short note explained that the seven-minute setting was never meant to be permanent. It had become a symbol of neighbours helping one another through a difficult timetable change.")),
            StoryPart(7,"Why Seven Minutes",listOf("Ada returned to Mr Okafor and showed him the signatures. He admitted that he had kept his workshop clocks slow because the old station clock had helped his father reach work during a transport strike.","He had wanted someone to discover the story rather than simply hear an explanation. The blue bird note was his invitation to investigate the people behind the clocks.")),
            StoryPart(8,"A Public Time",listOf("The museum board agreed to reopen a small display about the railway volunteers. They invited former staff, families, and the clockmakers' guild to verify every date before publishing the history.","Ada helped prepare a timeline that distinguished the temporary seven-minute adjustment from the later official time. The curator insisted that the display explain both the practical reason and the community memory.")),
            StoryPart(9,"The Opening",listOf("On opening day, Mr Okafor placed the restored clock face in its case. The former station manager spoke about the volunteers who had kept information moving when the announcement system failed.","Ada noticed that every guest had brought a photograph or a story. The museum collected copies with permission, creating an archive that belonged to the people who had lived the events.")),
            StoryPart(10,"Time, Corrected",listOf("The station clock was restored to accurate time, while the seven-minute setting was preserved in the exhibit as a documented chapter of local history. Mr Okafor finally set every workshop clock correctly.","Ada left with her watch repaired and a new appreciation for careful records. The mystery had not been about stolen time at all; it was about a useful temporary decision that outlived its purpose. The town now remembered both the facts and the kindness behind them."))
        )),
        StorySeries("the_map_of_small_things","The Map of Small Things","A class project turns overlooked neighbourhood places into a shared treasure.", listOf(
            StoryPart(1,"A Blank Map",listOf("For geography week, Malik's class received a blank map of their neighbourhood. The assignment was to mark important places, but Malik thought the usual landmarks left out the places that mattered most.","His grandmother suggested he begin with the bench where neighbours exchanged books, the tap where people shared water during repairs, and the little shade tree beside the bus stop.")),
            StoryPart(2,"The Red Pin",listOf("Malik marked the bench with a red pin. A note had been carved beneath it: 'If you found a book here, leave one for someone else.' Nobody knew who had started the exchange.","A shopkeeper remembered a teacher who used to bring books on Fridays. The teacher had retired, but her former school kept a box of community project photographs.")),
            StoryPart(3,"Photographs in a Box",listOf("The photographs showed children reading beneath the tree and a handwritten list of donated books. The list included a second location: a small courtyard behind the old clinic.","Malik visited with his teacher's permission and found the courtyard had become a shared herb garden. A volunteer there remembered the book exchange and explained that it had moved when the bench was repaired.")),
            StoryPart(4,"The Courtyard Garden",listOf("The garden volunteer showed Malik a notebook recording each group's contribution. A page listed the names of families who had helped build the first reading shelf.","One name belonged to Malik's grandmother. She had donated the shelf but had never mentioned it. Malik asked her why, and she said the project had always been about sharing, not recognition.")),
            StoryPart(5,"A Missing Place",listOf("The class map still had one unexplained symbol: a small yellow square beside the drainage canal. Malik's grandmother said it marked a footbridge that had been removed after heavy rain.","The city works office confirmed the bridge had been replaced by a safer crossing farther along the road. The old symbol was still useful because it showed where residents once met and helped one another.")),
            StoryPart(6,"Listening Walk",listOf("Malik's teacher organised a supervised listening walk. Residents explained which paths were accessible, where streetlights needed attention, and which public spaces were welcoming to children and older neighbours.","The class recorded suggestions rather than publishing private details. They checked every location with the people who used it and removed one pin after its owner asked to keep the place private.")),
            StoryPart(7,"The Map Changes",listOf("Malik added symbols for the book bench, garden, public crossing, and community noticeboard. Each symbol included a short note about who maintained the place and how visitors could help respectfully.","A classmate suggested adding the small repair kiosk where a mechanic fixed wheelchairs and bicycles. The mechanic agreed, provided the map did not promise services he could not always provide.")),
            StoryPart(8,"A Map for Everyone",listOf("The class made a large printed map and a simple digital version for the school website. They included a date, a correction contact, and a reminder to ask permission before photographing people or private property.","Residents reviewed the draft at a school meeting. They corrected two street names and suggested adding opening hours only when the organisations confirmed them.")),
            StoryPart(9,"The Neighbourhood Day",listOf("At the community day, families used the map to visit the garden, book bench, and noticeboard. Malik's grandmother brought the original shelf photograph and explained how the project had grown over time.","The class collected corrections and new suggestions in a public notebook. They did not treat the map as finished; they treated it as a shared record that needed care.")),
            StoryPart(10,"The Small Things Matter",listOf("The school adopted a yearly map review, with students checking details alongside residents and removing anything that was outdated or shared without permission. The book exchange gained a fresh shelf and a rotating volunteer team.","Malik pinned the first blank map beside the finished version. The neighbourhood's treasure had never been hidden underground. It was made of small acts of care, remembered accurately and made easier for others to join."))
        )),
        StorySeries("the_second_bell","The Second Bell","A school bell rings twice, leading students to an overlooked piece of school history.", listOf(
            StoryPart(1,"An Extra Ring",listOf("At exactly noon, the school bell rang twice. The first ring was normal; the second came ten seconds later and surprised everyone in the courtyard.","The headteacher checked the control panel, but the system showed only one scheduled signal. In the maintenance log, a handwritten note read: 'Second bell—do not erase until understood.'")),
            StoryPart(2,"The Old Logbook",listOf("The logbook belonged to a caretaker who had retired years earlier. His daughter, now a science teacher, remembered that he used to test the bell manually on special assembly days.","She found an old school newsletter describing a student radio club that had broadcast a second signal to announce lost property and urgent timetable changes.")),
            StoryPart(3,"A Radio in Storage",listOf("The radio club's equipment was stored in a locked cabinet. With permission, the teacher and two students inspected it and found a small timer connected to an obsolete speaker circuit.","A label on the timer carried the initials of two students, Efe and Jonah. The yearbook showed they had built the system as a practical project.")),
            StoryPart(4,"Two Inventors",listOf("Efe was now an electrician and Jonah worked in education. Both were surprised that the second bell still sounded. They explained that their prototype had been designed to ring only when the main signal failed.","They had presented it at a school exhibition, but the final wiring diagram was never added to the maintenance file. The device was later disconnected, or so they believed.")),
            StoryPart(5,"The Safe Inspection",listOf("The school arranged for a qualified technician to inspect the circuit before anyone touched it. The technician found an old backup relay that had recently been triggered by a failing component.","The second bell was not a secret message. It was the backup system doing exactly what the students had designed it to do, though the old component now needed replacement.")),
            StoryPart(6,"A Better Alarm",listOf("The technician replaced the worn component and tested the bell system under school safety procedures. The students watched from the designated area and recorded the results for their project report.","Efe and Jonah offered to help the school document the system, but the headteacher asked them to focus on explaining the design rather than making unauthorised changes.")),
            StoryPart(7,"The Forgotten Credit",listOf("While preparing the report, the students found that the old newsletter credited the radio club but not its two inventors. The yearbook and exhibition certificate confirmed both names.","The school archive committee agreed to correct the record. Efe said he cared less about an award than about future students knowing that a simple backup system could be an important engineering lesson.")),
            StoryPart(8,"A New Demonstration",listOf("The science club created a safe demonstration model using a low-voltage kit. It showed how a backup signal could alert staff when a primary system needed attention.","The students included a clear warning that real school alarm systems must be installed and maintained by qualified professionals. Their model was educational, not a replacement for the school's equipment.")),
            StoryPart(9,"The Assembly",listOf("At assembly, the headteacher thanked the former students and announced the corrected archive entry. The science club demonstrated its small model and explained the difference between a backup signal and a scheduled bell.","The school also created a maintenance record template so future caretakers could document changes, inspections, and test results clearly.")),
            StoryPart(10,"One Ring, Two Lessons",listOf("The bell now rang once at noon, while the backup circuit remained documented and properly maintained. The second ring became a story the science club told during its annual open day.","The students learned that a strange sound can be a clue, but evidence and qualified checks matter more than guesses. The school had recovered two inventors' names and a useful lesson in responsible engineering."))
        )),
        StorySeries("the_orange_envelope","The Orange Envelope","A community theatre group discovers the missing final page of its first play.", listOf(
            StoryPart(1,"The Unfinished Script",listOf("The community theatre planned to revive its first production, but the script ended halfway through the final scene. An orange envelope in the costume cupboard contained a note: 'The ending was never lost; it was waiting for the right cast.'","Director Sade asked the young performers to research the group's history before inventing a new ending. The envelope's postmark was twenty-five years old.")),
            StoryPart(2,"The First Cast",listOf("An old programme listed the original cast and a playwright named Mrs Akin. The theatre's records showed that she had moved away before the final performance.","A former actor remembered that Mrs Akin had written several endings and wanted the cast to choose one together. The production closed early when the hall needed urgent repairs.")),
            StoryPart(3,"Three Endings",listOf("The former actor had kept photocopies of three possible final scenes. Each ended differently: one with a reunion, one with a new beginning, and one with the characters choosing to work together.","None was marked final. Sade realised the missing page might not be a single answer; the playwright may have wanted the cast to decide which ending best fit their performance.")),
            StoryPart(4,"A Letter to the Group",listOf("The theatre archive contained a letter from Mrs Akin thanking the cast for helping shape the play. It said the final scene should be chosen after the actors understood the characters' choices.","The letter included a forwarding address for a literary society. Sade wrote a polite enquiry and attached a scan of the programme, asking whether Mrs Akin could be contacted.")),
            StoryPart(5,"The Playwright Replies",listOf("Mrs Akin replied with a voice message and permission to share her memories. She explained that she had taken the pages home while the hall was repaired, then misplaced the envelope during a move.","She still had her original notes, but she did not want the group to treat them as an instruction. She encouraged the new cast to read all three endings and choose honestly.")),
            StoryPart(6,"What the Characters Need",listOf("The performers discussed the story's central question: whether the characters should wait for an apology or begin rebuilding their town together. They listed what each character had learned throughout the play.","Sade noticed that the reunion ending ignored one character's need for independence. The new beginning ending gave everyone a voice, while the teamwork ending offered a practical path forward.")),
            StoryPart(7,"A Fourth Page",listOf("Mrs Akin sent a fourth page she had drafted years later. It combined the best parts of the earlier endings but left the final decision to the characters themselves.","The cast rehearsed it and found that the scene felt rushed. They asked permission to make small changes while preserving the playwright's themes and credit.")),
            StoryPart(8,"The Rehearsal Room",listOf("The actors revised the scene with Mrs Akin's approval. They added pauses, clarified why the characters trusted one another again, and made the final choice something the audience could understand.","The costume team found the original orange envelope's matching label in a box of programmes. The group displayed it beside the script as a reminder of the play's long journey.")),
            StoryPart(9,"Opening Night",listOf("The revived play opened to a full community hall. The ending was neither a perfect reunion nor a sudden solution; it showed the characters listening, setting fair boundaries, and agreeing on one shared task.","After the performance, the audience stayed for a discussion about how stories change when new people bring their own experiences. Mrs Akin joined by video and thanked the cast for treating the work with care.")),
            StoryPart(10,"The Ending They Chose",listOf("The theatre archived the original drafts, the approved revised page, and a note explaining which changes belonged to the new cast. Mrs Akin was credited as playwright, with the revival team credited for its adaptation.","Sade placed the orange envelope in a display case. Its message had not promised a hidden perfect ending. It had invited the next generation to understand the characters and make a thoughtful choice. The play was complete at last, and its history remained honest."))
        )),
        StorySeries("the_rain_library","The Rain Library","A rainy-day reading room brings a scattered collection and its donors back together.", listOf(
            StoryPart(1,"Books After the Storm",listOf("After a heavy storm damaged the community centre roof, volunteers moved its books into temporary boxes. When the room reopened, one box was missing and the handwritten catalogue had a blank page where its number should have been.","Lara, a student volunteer, found a paper raindrop tucked into the catalogue. It carried a shelf code and the words: 'Look where the rain was measured.'")),
            StoryPart(2,"The Rain Gauge",listOf("The community garden kept a rain gauge beside the tool shed. Beneath its wooden stand, Lara found a waterproof list of book titles and the initials of several donors.","The garden coordinator explained that the reading room had once held outdoor story sessions. The missing box might have been moved during those events, but nobody remembered where.")),
            StoryPart(3,"A Donor's List",listOf("Lara matched the initials to the library's donor register. One belonged to a retired nurse who had donated children's books; another belonged to a carpenter who built the original shelves.","The nurse remembered a travelling book cart that visited the clinic waiting room. She suggested checking the clinic's community cupboard, with the manager's permission.")),
            StoryPart(4,"The Travelling Cart",listOf("The clinic manager found an old photograph of the cart, but the missing box was not there. A note on the back mentioned that the cart had been borrowed by a family literacy group.","The group met at a nearby school. Its organiser remembered returning the cart, but said one box had been sent to a storage room when the centre was renovated.")),
            StoryPart(5,"The Storage Number",listOf("The school inventory listed a box with the same shelf code, but its description simply said 'mixed reading materials'. Lara asked the school librarian to verify the contents before moving anything.","Inside were the missing books, a reading-session notebook, and a list of families who had requested large-print copies. The box had been stored safely but its label had faded.")),
            StoryPart(6,"The Unfinished Catalogue",listOf("The notebook showed that the volunteers had planned to create a lending service for people unable to visit the centre. The project stopped when the roof damage forced the room to close.","Lara asked the donors whether they still wanted to support the idea. Several offered time, while the carpenter volunteered to repair the book cart after checking its condition.")),
            StoryPart(7,"Making It Accessible",listOf("The team designed a simple sign-out system and asked readers which formats worked best for them. They added large-print labels and made a list of books available in audio format through authorised library services.","The volunteers agreed not to publish borrowers' names. The catalogue would record titles and availability, while private lending details stayed with the library staff.")),
            StoryPart(8,"The First Route",listOf("The repaired cart made its first visit to the clinic and the senior centre. Volunteers read aloud, helped visitors request books, and recorded suggestions for the next route.","A rainy afternoon brought more visitors than expected. Lara learned that the service needed a rota, a dry storage place, and a clear process for returning books.")),
            StoryPart(9,"A Room Reopened",listOf("The community centre held a reopening day with a display of the recovered books and the original rain-session notebook. Donors saw their contributions together for the first time in years.","The organisers announced a monthly travelling-cart schedule and invited residents to suggest accessible locations. The service would be reviewed each term so it could adapt to real needs.")),
            StoryPart(10,"A Library That Travels",listOf("The missing box was returned to the shelves, and the travelling cart became a permanent part of the community library. The blank catalogue page was filled with a clear note explaining the box's journey.","Lara kept the paper raindrop beside the catalogue. The clue had led to more than a missing collection: it had restored a promise to make reading reachable. The rain had moved the books, but neighbours had brought the library back together."))
        ))
    )
}
